  {
    "episode_id": 90932,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90932.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 13,
    "turn_text": "Well, I paint the picture of where we are right now. That's the prologue to the book, is we're in a state of tribalism. No one likes that word when Amy Chuck came out with it, right? It incited some people, but this idea of dividing up America used to be a supergroup. We used to be, you know, people who would migrate to the United States would have the goal of being Americans and then they would be, you know, their ethnicity or whatever social economic group they divided into would be second. And that's sort of flipped over the past decades. Whether it's ideology or race or economic status or whatever, we are now placing that above being an American. And that tribalism is, you can see it everywhere. It's at the water cooler at work. It's at the checkout line at the grocery store. Americans are just sort of at each other's throat over politics, which never was the case. And of course, the ideologies themselves have drifted further and further apart. so amplified by social media amplified by uh... the ability to see news in seconds after it happens The psychology that really has been there since the dawn of man. I make the point in the book that this division or tendency of human beings to divide has been present since Cain and Abel. The herdsmen or the rowcropper, they divided it, resulted in a murder. So we have to fight that. and get back to being Americans and making that more important than our own personal quest. And so we before me, that's kind of where we are right now. We're in this state of division. I think there's a lot of question marks in people's minds right now because of the election. So that people are saying, are the sort of tenants that we've lived by, the values that we've held, You know, still popular. And I think on the left, they're having to ask that question of themselves. There's a lot of introspection going on there because this was a pretty resounding victory for the right. But when you look at it politically, up until this point, and that newfound sort of introspection, we're pretty divided.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_08",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "The current state of America is characterized by tribalism.",
        "confidence": 0.97
      },
      {
        "text": "Currently, ethnic, racial, ideological, or economic identities are placed above national identity.",
        "confidence": 0.96
      },
      {
        "text": "In the past, immigrants prioritized becoming Americans over maintaining ethnic or socioeconomic identities.",
