    "turn_text": "Sure, my dad lost his arm when I was in first grade to cancer, to bone cancer. And he had acquired that strongly, believe, from exposure in the Air Force. He was a nuclear weapons technician. This was, you know, Berlin crisis time, post-Berlin crisis time. And they couldn't arm the nuclear weapon from inside the aircraft. So he had to stick his arm into the nuclear weapon three times a day, turn it on, turn it off, and wait for the war to happen. Thank God it never did. And Dad got to come home, but he came home. The Air Force discovered something, discharged him, didn't tell him what it was. Nine years later, he loses his arm. And the company he worked for, this was pre-Americans with Disabilities Act, said you're half a man, we're paying you half a salary. So dad lost half his salary, half his income, and his right dominant right arm in six months. And my father refused to take assistance from anyone, really, and went out and roofed houses with one arm. And if you can imagine, you know, when you start a nail, normally you hold a nail and start a nail, you know, this was pre-nail gun. So my father had to basically start every nail with the palm of his hand. And we would roof houses this way. I was in first grade. I was his helper. And so I learned that You know, you don't ask for a handout, you go work, and I learn to work hard. But I also learned, my dad taught me that, you know, the whole is more important than us as individuals. We grew up in a small town in Mississippi, dirt road. The reputation of the family could impact any one of us for years to come. And that was ingrained in me that the whole we is bigger than who I am, me. And Dad taught me that from a very early age. You know, those are the things that were planted in service to others. That was sort of the other thing that my parents, my dad with one arm would go and cut firewood for other people who had two, but were unable to cut it because of some illness or something like that. And I'd be like, Dad, this doesn't make sense to me. That guy has two arms. You have one. Why are we going to cut firewood for him? And of course, dad's like, he needs it. We're going to go do it. So service and hard work and the whole is more important than myself as an",
