  {
    "episode_id": 90932,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90932.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 84,
    "turn_text": "You know, I went on a... Well, first, when I came up here, I started this thing called the Reagan O'Neill Club. And the Reagan O'Neill Club was an idea I had to help heal the division. So I went to Haley Stevens, who was my fellow class president on the Democrat side. I'm on the Republican side as the freshman class president. Hey, let's start this club. No politics, no policy, just relationship. And so we started with eight Republicans, eight Democrats. It went pretty well. COVID happens and it sort of falls apart. And so I tried to get it back going after we could get back together and it sort of stuttered. So I'm thinking, okay, this is going to die. Well, I went on a congressional delegation, three Republicans, three Democrats. First dinner, Republicans over here, Democrats over here. Second dinner, Republicans over here, Democrats over here. The next day I was driving in the van and a congresswoman from Massachusetts is sitting in front of me and says something that really frustrated me. And so I go up, I'm thinking to myself, if I dislike this person because of what they've said, I'm disliking the 800,000, 700,000 Americans who sent her to this office. and I can't do that having been a military guy. So I go up and I start asking questions, you know, where are you from? Okay. Tell me about her husband. He's a home builder, American dream, right? She was a Hall of Fame basketball player or volleyball player at Georgetown. You all will be able to figure out who this is pretty quickly. But I started developing respect for her. You know, I didn't agree with her, but respect. And so I came back with a renewed sense. We've got to get the Reagan O'Neill Club. Now it's like 30 Republicans and 30 Democrats. And I mean, people want to join. So there is in the Congress, a desire to not always agree, but to stop, you know, communicating hatred for one another, which is what social media wants us to do, because likes are a drug. And if I can be more bombastic, I'll get more likes and shares. And so we have to overcome that and that tendency that, like I said, has been there since the dawn of time and fight to be America again. And I think there are people who want to do that. And I certainly do.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_08",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
