  {
    "episode_id": 90932,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/0/90932.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 127,
    "turn_text": "So it's interesting you say that, and the question becomes what sort of broader swath of America that we all represent here in this building. So we started here. Can we expand this idea and this notion to the rest of the country? That's what you're asking. And I was at the University of Tennessee speaking at the Baker Center, you know, after a great Tennessee in Senator Baker. And I'm talking about this divide in the country. And I talk about the Reagan O'Neill Club. And after I'm done, both the faculty and the students want to do something. They want to start something. And we've got to kind of, we decided to hold off on this at this level yet. But at some point, we may go down and create you know, Reagan O'Neill clubs all over the country at universities. Again, with the idea that we can disagree, we shouldn't hate each other because we disagree. This idea of relationship and mutual respect, despite disagreement, and hopefully we can get that into college campuses again because let's face it, they've gone off the deep end on that.",
